::GEO_U05_L06_GP01::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L06_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A sector has radius 6 and central angle 60°. Which expression gives its arc length?
{
=A. 60/360(2pi6)#Arc length is the fraction of the circumference determined by the central angle: θ/360(2pir).
~B. 60/360(pi6?)#This is the sector area structure, not arc length.
~C. 2pi6#This is the full circumference, not the arc for a 60° sector.
~D. pi6?#This is the full circle area, not an arc length.
}

::GEO_U05_L06_GP02::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L06_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A sector has radius 8 and central angle 90°. Which expression gives its sector area?
{
~A. 90/360(2pi8)#This is the arc length structure, not sector area.
=B. 90/360(pi8?)#Sector area is the central-angle fraction of the circle area.
~C. 2pi8#This is circumference, not area.
~D. 90pi#This ignores the radius and the fraction of the circle.
}

::GEO_U05_L06_GP03::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L06_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A 120° arc has radius 9. What coefficient of pi gives the arc length?
{
~A. 9pi#This uses the radius but not the central-angle fraction.
~B. 120pi#This uses the angle as a length coefficient.
=C. 6pi#Use 120/360(2pi9), so the coefficient of pi is 6.
~D. Cannot be determined#The radius and central angle are given, so the arc length coefficient can be determined.
}

::GEO_U05_L06_GP04::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L06_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A 45° sector has radius 10. What coefficient of pi gives the sector area?
{
~A. 10pi#This uses the radius but does not square it or use the angle fraction.
~B. 45pi#This uses the angle as an area coefficient.
~C. 2.5pi#This coefficient comes from arc length, not sector area.
=D. 12.5pi#Use 45/360(pi10?), so the coefficient of pi is 12.5.
}

::GEO_U05_L06_GP05::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L06_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A sector has radius 5 and central angle 72°. Which expression gives its arc length?
{
=A. 72/360(2pi5)#Arc length is the fraction of the circumference determined by the central angle: θ/360(2pir).
~B. 72/360(pi5?)#This is the sector area structure, not arc length.
~C. 2pi5#This is the full circumference, not the arc for a 72° sector.
~D. pi5?#This is the full circle area, not an arc length.
}

